e2=A.a(["The risk ratio (RR) compares two risks: a/(a+b) divided by c/(c+d). It requires the group proportions to estimate risks: cohort, trial, or cross-sectional study (prevalence ratio).","The odds ratio (OR) compares two odds (odds = p/(1\u2212p)). It is the only measure estimable in a case-control study and the natural measure of logistic regression.",'When the outcome is rare (< 10%), OR \u2248 RR. When it is common, the OR is further from 1 than the RR: writing it as "risk multiplied by" exaggerates the effect.',"For an adjusted RR of a common outcome, Poisson regression with robust variance is an option; in a case-control study, only logistic regression (OR) is appropriate."],c5)
