g6=A.a(["Power is the probability of detecting the effect if it truly exists. It depends on four quantities: the effect to detect, the variability (or the event frequency), \u03b1, and the sample size. Fixing three gives the fourth.","Survival: power comes from the number of EVENTS, not of patients. Freedman or Schoenfeld give the number of deaths or relapses required; the number of patients follows from the probability of an event during the study (accrual, follow-up). A longer follow-up can replace patients.","Case-control: the effect is an OR; power depends on the frequency of exposure in controls. Adding controls per case helps, but beyond about 4 controls per case the gain becomes small.","GWAS: about one million independent tests are performed, hence the genome-wide threshold \u03b1 = 5\xb710\u207b\u2078. True effects of common variants are small (OR 1.05 to 1.2): thousands to tens of thousands of cases are needed.","Post hoc power, computed from the observed effect, adds nothing to the p-value: to interpret a non-significant result, look at the confidence interval."],c5)
