h6=A.a(["When the same subject is measured several times (visits, days, eyes, teeth), the measurements are correlated: a subject who starts high tends to stay high. Treating them as independent overstates the information and gives standard errors that are too small for between-subject comparisons.","The linear mixed model adds subject-specific random effects: a random intercept (each subject has his own level) and, if needed, a random slope (his own evolution over time). The fixed effects describe the average evolution and the differences between groups; the variances describe how much subjects differ.","It uses all available measurements, even from subjects who left the study, and remains valid if dropout depends on values already observed (MAR). Excluding incomplete subjects wastes information and can bias results.","In a trial, the treatment effect on the evolution is the time \xd7 treatment interaction: one test for the whole follow-up, instead of a comparison at each visit (multiple tests).","Alternative: GEE (generalized estimating equations) estimate a population-average effect with robust standard errors, without modelling individual trajectories."],c5)
