B.b5L=s(["Avec peu d'\xe9v\xe9nements, le maximum de vraisemblance surestime les coefficients et les IC de Wald couvrent mal.","S\xe9paration : une variable pr\xe9dit parfaitement l'issue dans un sous-groupe (p. ex. aucun cas dans une cat\xe9gorie). L'estimation tend vers l'infini ; un logiciel peut afficher un nombre absurde sans erreur.","La p\xe9nalisation de Firth donne des estimations finies et moins biais\xe9es, mais ne cr\xe9e pas d'information : l'incertitude reste grande. Ses IC et tests se lisent par vraisemblance profil\xe9e p\xe9nalis\xe9e, pas par la m\xe9thode de Wald.","Options : moins de covariables (choisies a priori), regroupement justifi\xe9, m\xe9thodes exactes, analyse descriptive, pr\xe9sentation exploratoire."],t.s)
B.bnP=s(["un effet tr\xe8s fort","une s\xe9paration","une colin\xe9arit\xe9 mod\xe9r\xe9e"],t.s)
B.agR=new A.b0("Un OR de 3,2 \xd7 10\u2078 avec un ES de 4 000 en sortie de logistique \xe9voque\u2026",B.bnP,1,null,0,"Coefficient et ES d\xe9mesur\xe9s : s\xe9paration.")
B.b7S=s([B.agR],t.Fe)
B.b7G=s(["Rapporter un OR \xab infini \xbb ou d\xe9mesur\xe9.","Ajouter 10 covariables pour 12 \xe9v\xe9nements."],t.s)
B.am5=new A.d0("small_events","Peu d'\xe9v\xe9nements, s\xe9paration et r\xe9gression de Firth",B.cd,B.b5L,"Transmission m\xe8re-enfant de l'AgHBs (donn\xe9es fictives)","2 enfants infect\xe9s sur 48 : l'IC exact de la proportion va d'environ 0,5 % \xe0 14 %. Croiser avec le statut AgHBe maternel (0 cas chez les m\xe8res AgHBe\u2212) produit une s\xe9paration : seule une analyse de Firth donne un OR fini. Son IC par vraisemblance profil\xe9e p\xe9nalis\xe9e, asym\xe9trique, est \xe0 pr\xe9f\xe9rer \xe0 l'IC de Wald, sym\xe9trique sur l'\xe9chelle log et trompeur avec si peu d'\xe9v\xe9nements.",B.b7S,B.b7G,"firth_logistic")
B.b8I=s(["Explicatif : estimer l'effet d'UNE exposition, en contr\xf4lant la confusion. Les covariables viennent d'un raisonnement causal ; on interpr\xe8te un coefficient.","Pr\xe9dictif : estimer au mieux un risque individuel. Les pr\xe9dicteurs doivent \xeatre disponibles au moment de la pr\xe9diction ; on juge la discrimination, la calibration et l'utilit\xe9, pas les coefficients.","Un bon pr\xe9dicteur n'est pas une cause (un sympt\xf4me pr\xe9dit la maladie) ; une cause peut mal pr\xe9dire.","M\xe9langer les deux objectifs conduit \xe0 des choix incoh\xe9rents (s\xe9lection pas \xe0 pas pour \xab expliquer \xbb, interpr\xe9tation causale d'un score)."],t.s)
B.bfP=s(["l'AUC du mod\xe8le","l'estimation ajust\xe9e de l'effet du tabac et son IC"],t.s)
B.agx=new A.b0("Objectif : savoir si le tabagisme augmente le risque d'HTA. Le crit\xe8re principal d'\xe9valuation est\u2026",B.bfP,1,null,0,"Question explicative.")
B.aXy=s([B.agx],t.Fe)
B.aDz=s(["Interpr\xe9ter les OR d'un score comme des effets causaux.","Juger un mod\xe8le explicatif sur son AUC."],t.s)
B.am6=new A.d0("explanatory_vs_predictive","Mod\xe8le explicatif ou mod\xe8le pr\xe9dictif ?",B.cd,B.b8I,"Tour de taille","Dans un score de risque, le tour de taille am\xe9liore la pr\xe9diction ; cela ne dit pas qu'une r\xe9duction du tour de taille r\xe9duirait le risque d'autant.",B.aXy,B.aDz,"logistic_regression")
B.b5H=s(["Discrimination (AUC, C-statistique) : capacit\xe9 \xe0 classer plus haut les sujets qui auront l'\xe9v\xe9nement. Calibration : accord entre risques pr\xe9dits et fr\xe9quences observ\xe9es.","Un mod\xe8le peut bien discriminer et \xeatre mal calibr\xe9 (pr\xe9dire 30 % quand on observe 10 %) : d\xe9cisions fond\xe9es sur des seuils fausses.","Pente de calibration < 1 : pr\xe9dictions trop extr\xeames, signe de surajustement. La validation interne par bootstrap estime cet optimisme.","Validation interne \u2260 validation externe \u2260 utilit\xe9 clinique. Aucun mod\xe8le n'est \xab valid\xe9 cliniquement \xbb sur sa seule AUC.","En validation externe, les coefficients sont appliqu\xe9s SANS r\xe9estimation. Un intercept de calibration \u2260 0 (O/E \u2260 1) appelle une mise \xe0 jour de l'intercept ; une pente < 1, une recalibration logistique ; la version recalibr\xe9e doit \xe0 son tour \xeatre \xe9valu\xe9e.","Le test de Hosmer-Lemeshow compare \xe9v\xe9nements observ\xe9s et attendus par d\xe9ciles de risque pr\xe9dit : peu puissant sur petit effectif, il signale des \xe9carts n\xe9gligeables sur grand effectif. Juger plut\xf4t sur la courbe de calibration liss\xe9e, la pente et l'intercept."],t.s)
B.biR=s(["Utiliser le mod\xe8le tel quel.","R\xe9duire le surajustement (moins de param\xe8tres, r\xe9tr\xe9cissement) puis valider en externe."],t.s)
B.agp=new A.b0("AUC corrig\xe9e 0,84, pente corrig\xe9e 0,78. Que faire avant usage ?",B.biR,1,null,0,"Pente 0,78 : pr\xe9dictions trop extr\xeames ; la validation externe reste n\xe9cessaire.")
B.bbc=s([B.agp],t.Fe)
B.bim=s(["Rapporter seulement l'AUC.","Partager al\xe9atoirement l'\xe9chantillon et appeler cela \xab validation externe \xbb.","Choisir le seuil sur les m\xeames donn\xe9es sans le dire."],t.s)
B.am7=new A.d0("calibration","Discrimination et calibration",B.cd,B.b5H,"Mod\xe8le cardiom\xe9tabolique (d\xe9monstration 3)","Voir la d\xe9monstration : AUC apparente, AUC corrig\xe9e de l'optimisme, pente corrig\xe9e et courbe de d\xe9cision.",B.bbc,B.bim,"prediction_logistic_internal")
B.bh6=s(["With few events, maximum likelihood overestimates coefficients and Wald CIs have poor coverage.","Separation: a variable perfectly predicts the outcome in a subgroup (e.g. no case in a category). The estimate tends to infinity; software may display an absurd number without an error.","Firth's penalization gives finite and less biased estimates, but does not create information: uncertainty remains large. Its CIs and tests are read by penalized profile likelihood, not by the Wald method.","Options: fewer covariates (chosen a priori), justified merging, exact methods, descriptive analysis, exploratory presentation."],t.s)
B.bgB=s(["a very strong effect","separation","moderate collinearity"],t.s)
B.ahH=new A.b0("An OR of 3.2 \xd7 10\u2078 with an SE of 4,000 in a logistic output suggests\u2026",B.bgB,1,null,0,"Excessive coefficient and SE: separation.")
B.b9M=s([B.ahH],t.Fe)
B.b7A=s(['Reporting an "infinite" or excessive OR.',"Adding 10 covariates for 12 events."],t.s)
B.am8=new A.d0("small_events","Few events, separation and Firth's regression",B.cd,B.bh6,"Mother-to-child transmission of HBsAg (fictitious data)","2 infected children out of 48: the exact CI of the proportion runs from about 0.5% to 14%. Crossing with maternal HBeAg status (0 cases among HBeAg\u2212 mothers) produces separation: only a Firth analysis gives a finite OR. Its asymmetric penalized profile likelihood CI is to be preferred to the Wald CI, which is symmetric on the log scale and misleading with so few events.",B.b9M,B.b7A,"firth_logistic")
B.bdX=s(["Explanatory model: covariates are chosen a priori from a causal diagram (DAG): confounders yes, mediators and colliders no.","Stepwise selection on p-values produces biased coefficients, CIs that are too narrow and non-reproducible results.","The number of parameters must remain compatible with the sample size (events per parameter in logistic regression).",'Only the coefficient of the exposure of interest is interpreted; the coefficients of adjustment covariates have no causal interpretation ("Table 2 fallacy").',"A continuous variable should not be cut into classes (loss of information, arbitrary cut-offs). If the relationship may be curved, a restricted cubic spline (3 to 5 knots fixed in advance) lets the data draw the shape; read the curve and the nonlinearity test, not the individual coefficients."],t.s)
B.bj5=s(["correct.","too narrow, because they ignore the selection step."],t.s)
B.agL=new A.b0("A stepwise regression kept 4 variables out of 15. The reported CIs are\u2026",B.bj5,1,null,0,"The uncertainty of the selection is not accounted for.")
B.bnN=s([B.agL],t.Fe)
B.bnn=s(["Selection on p < 0.20 in univariable analysis.","Interpreting all ORs in the table as effects."],t.s)
B.am9=new A.d0("covariates","Choosing the covariates of a model",B.cd,B.bdX,"Smoking and hypertension","For the effect of smoking on hypertension: adjust for age and sex (confounding), not for the SBP at the previous visit (close to the outcome).",B.bnN,B.bnn,"linear_regression")
B.b7_=s(["Mod\xe8le explicatif : les covariables se choisissent a priori \xe0 partir d'un sch\xe9ma causal (DAG) : facteurs de confusion oui, m\xe9diateurs et colliders non.","La s\xe9lection pas \xe0 pas sur les valeurs de p produit des coefficients biais\xe9s, des IC trop \xe9troits et des r\xe9sultats non reproductibles.","Le nombre de param\xe8tres doit rester compatible avec l'effectif (\xe9v\xe9nements par param\xe8tre en logistique).","Seul le coefficient de l'exposition d'int\xe9r\xeat s'interpr\xe8te ; les coefficients des covariables d'ajustement n'ont pas d'interpr\xe9tation causale (sophisme de la \xab table 2 \xbb).","Une variable continue ne doit pas \xeatre d\xe9coup\xe9e en classes (perte d'information, seuils arbitraires). Si la relation peut \xeatre courbe, une spline cubique restreinte (3 \xe0 5 n\u0153uds fix\xe9s \xe0 l'avance) laisse les donn\xe9es dessiner la forme ; on lit la courbe et le test de non-lin\xe9arit\xe9, pas les coefficients isol\xe9s."],t.s)
B.b83=s(["corrects.","trop \xe9troits, car ils ignorent l'\xe9tape de s\xe9lection."],t.s)
B.ah7=new A.b0("Une r\xe9gression pas \xe0 pas a retenu 4 variables sur 15. Les IC rapport\xe9s sont\u2026",B.b83,1,null,0,"L'incertitude de la s\xe9lection n'est pas prise en compte.")
B.bbJ=s([B.ah7],t.Fe)
B.blU=s(["S\xe9lection sur p < 0,20 en analyse univari\xe9e.","Interpr\xe9ter tous les OR du tableau comme des effets."],t.s)
B.ama=new A.d0("covariates","Choisir les covariables d'un mod\xe8le",B.cd,B.b7_,"Tabac et HTA","Pour l'effet du tabac sur l'HTA : ajuster sur l'\xe2ge et le sexe (confusion), pas sur la PAS de la visite pr\xe9c\xe9dente (proche de l'issue).",B.bbJ,B.blU,"linear_regression")
B.ef=new A.F8(1,"intermediate")
B.bk0=s(["Deux mesures sur les m\xeames sujets (avant/apr\xe8s, deux yeux, deux tests sur le m\xeame pr\xe9l\xe8vement) ne sont pas ind\xe9pendantes : l'analyse porte sur les diff\xe9rences intra-sujet.","Quantitatif : t appari\xe9 (diff\xe9rences ~ normales) ou Wilcoxon sign\xe9. Binaire : McNemar, qui n'utilise que les paires discordantes.","Sans groupe t\xe9moin, un changement avant/apr\xe8s n'est pas un effet du traitement (r\xe9gression vers la moyenne, \xe9volution naturelle)."],t.s)
B.Q_=s(["100","19","81"],t.s)
B.agS=new A.b0("Combien de paires sont utilis\xe9es par le test de McNemar dans l'exemple ?",B.Q_,1,null,0,"14 + 5 = 19 paires discordantes.")
B.bg4=s([B.agS],t.Fe)
B.bdj=s(["Analyser deux temps de mesure comme deux groupes ind\xe9pendants.","Exclure silencieusement les paires incompl\xe8tes."],t.s)
B.amb=new A.d0("paired","Donn\xe9es appari\xe9es",B.ef,B.bk0,"Deux tests sur 100 pr\xe9l\xe8vements","40 positifs aux deux, 41 n\xe9gatifs aux deux, 14 positifs seulement au test A, 5 seulement au test B : seules les 19 paires discordantes informent la comparaison.",B.bg4,B.bdj,"mcnemar")
B.bex=s(["MCAR : manque sans lien avec les donn\xe9es ; MAR : li\xe9 aux variables observ\xe9es ; MNAR : li\xe9 \xe0 la valeur manquante elle-m\xeame.","L'analyse en cas complets est sans biais sous MCAR (et dans certains cas MAR) mais perd de la puissance ; elle peut \xeatre biais\xe9e sinon.","Toujours rapporter le nombre de manquants par variable et comparer inclus et exclus.","L'imputation multiple est la r\xe9f\xe9rence sous MAR : m jeux compl\xe9t\xe9s, une analyse par jeu, combinaison par les r\xe8gles de Rubin (la variance entre imputations traduit l'incertitude due aux manquants). Inclure l'issue dans le mod\xe8le d'imputation. Une imputation simple par la moyenne sous-estime la variance.","Pour un mod\xe8le de pr\xe9diction, l'imputation doit \xeatre refaite dans chaque r\xe9plique bootstrap ; sinon la validation interne r\xe9utilise une information issue de l'ensemble des donn\xe9es."],t.s)
B.bm5=s(["est sans cons\xe9quence.","r\xe9duit artificiellement la variance et peut biaiser les associations."],t.s)
B.ahF=new A.b0("Remplacer les valeurs manquantes par la moyenne\u2026",B.bm5,1,null,0,"Imputation simple : pr\xe9cision illusoire.")
B.baj=s([B.ahF],t.Fe)
B.b9W=s(["Ne pas mentionner les exclusions.","Imputer l'issue en pr\xe9diction sans le d\xe9clarer."],t.s)
B.amc=new A.d0("missing","Donn\xe9es manquantes",B.ef,B.bex,"Glyc\xe9mie manquante","Si la glyc\xe9mie manque surtout chez les patients vus en urgence, l'hypoth\xe8se MCAR est peu plausible.",B.baj,B.b9W,"describe_numeric")
B.bcZ=s(["On calcule pour chaque sujet la diff\xe9rence entre m\xe9thodes. Le biais est la moyenne des diff\xe9rences ; les limites d'accord (biais \xb1 1,96 \xc9T) contiennent environ 95 % des diff\xe9rences individuelles.","Les limites d'accord ne sont PAS un intervalle de confiance : elles d\xe9crivent la dispersion des individus. L'IC du biais d\xe9crit la pr\xe9cision de la moyenne ; chaque limite a aussi son propre IC.","L'acceptabilit\xe9 se juge contre un \xe9cart cliniquement tol\xe9rable fix\xe9 AVANT l'analyse.","Si la diff\xe9rence augmente avec le niveau mesur\xe9 (biais proportionnel), analyser le rapport ou le logarithme.","Pour comparer une nouvelle m\xe9thode de dosage \xe0 la m\xe9thode de r\xe9f\xe9rence, la r\xe9gression de Passing-Bablok (non param\xe9trique) ou de Deming (erreur sur les deux m\xe9thodes) s\xe9pare le biais constant (ordonn\xe9e \u2260 0) du biais proportionnel (pente \u2260 1) ; on conclut sur le biais aux niveaux de d\xe9cision cliniques, compar\xe9 \xe0 un biais acceptable fix\xe9 a priori."],t.s)
B.bab=s(["que le biais vrai est entre \u22120,9 et 1,3 avec 95 % de confiance.","qu'environ 95 % des diff\xe9rences individuelles sont attendues entre \u22120,9 et 1,3."],t.s)
B.ahv=new A.b0("\xab Limites d'accord \xe0 95 % : \u22120,9 \xe0 1,3 \xbb signifie\u2026",B.bab,1,null,0,"Les limites d'accord d\xe9crivent les individus ; l'IC du biais d\xe9crit la moyenne.")
B.aWm=s([B.ahv],t.Fe)
B.az4=s(["Appeler les limites d'accord \xab intervalle de confiance \xbb.","Juger l'accord sans crit\xe8re clinique a priori."],t.s)
B.amd=new A.d0("agreement","Accord entre m\xe9thodes : Bland-Altman",B.ef,B.bcZ,"Glyc\xe9mie capillaire vs veineuse","Biais = +0,2 mmol/L (IC 0,05 \xe0 0,35) ; limites d'accord \u22120,9 \xe0 +1,3 mmol/L : un patient peut pr\xe9senter un \xe9cart de 1,3 mmol/L, ce qu'un IC du biais \xe9troit ne montre pas.",B.aWm,B.az4,"bland_altman")
B.ee=new A.F8(0,"beginner")
B.bod=s(["Le r de Pearson mesure l'intensit\xe9 d'une relation LIN\xc9AIRE ; le \u03c1 de Spearman celle d'une relation monotone.","Une corr\xe9lation forte ne prouve ni la causalit\xe9, ni l'accord entre deux m\xe9thodes (deux m\xe9thodes peuvent \xeatre parfaitement corr\xe9l\xe9es et diff\xe9rer de 20 %).","Toujours regarder le nuage de points : une valeur extr\xeame peut cr\xe9er ou masquer une corr\xe9lation."],t.s)
B.bfV=s(["r de Pearson","Bland-Altman","t de Welch"],t.s)
B.agj=new A.b0("Pour juger si deux appareils sont interchangeables, on utilise\u2026",B.bfV,1,null,0,"L'accord s'\xe9tudie sur les diff\xe9rences (Bland-Altman).")
B.bbr=s([B.agj],t.Fe)
B.b6W=s(["Conclure \xe0 l'accord \xe0 partir de r.","Interpr\xe9ter r = 0 comme absence de toute relation (relation non lin\xe9aire possible)."],t.s)
B.ame=new A.d0("correlation","Corr\xe9lation : ce qu'elle dit et ne dit pas",B.ee,B.bod,"Cr\xe9atinine par deux automates","Si l'automate B donne toujours 1,2 \xd7 la valeur de A, r = 1 alors que les r\xe9sultats ne sont pas interchangeables.",B.bbr,B.b6W,"pearson")
B.bnG=s(["Explanatory: estimate the effect of ONE exposure, controlling confounding. Covariates come from causal reasoning; one coefficient is interpreted.","Predictive: estimate an individual risk as well as possible. Predictors must be available at the time of prediction; discrimination, calibration and usefulness are judged, not the coefficients.","A good predictor is not a cause (a symptom predicts the disease); a cause may predict poorly.",'Mixing the two aims leads to inconsistent choices (stepwise selection to "explain", causal interpretation of a score).'],t.s)
B.bdh=s(["the AUC of the model","the adjusted estimate of the effect of smoking and its CI"],t.s)
B.agl=new A.b0("Aim: to find out whether smoking increases the risk of hypertension. The main evaluation criterion is\u2026",B.bdh,1,null,0,"Explanatory question.")
B.bdU=s([B.agl],t.Fe)
B.boA=s(["Interpreting the ORs of a score as causal effects.","Judging an explanatory model on its AUC."],t.s)
B.amf=new A.d0("explanatory_vs_predictive","Explanatory or predictive model?",B.cd,B.bnG,"Waist circumference","In a risk score, waist circumference improves prediction; this does not mean that reducing waist circumference would reduce the risk by as much.",B.bdU,B.boA,"logistic_regression")
B.blX=s(["Discrimination (AUC, C-statistic): ability to rank higher the subjects who will have the event. Calibration: agreement between predicted risks and observed frequencies.","A model can discriminate well and be poorly calibrated (predicting 30% when 10% is observed): threshold-based decisions are then wrong.","Calibration slope < 1: predictions too extreme, a sign of overfitting. Internal validation by bootstrap estimates this optimism.",'Internal validation \u2260 external validation \u2260 clinical usefulness. No model is "clinically validated" on its AUC alone.',"In external validation, the coefficients are applied WITHOUT re-estimation. A calibration intercept \u2260 0 (O/E \u2260 1) calls for an intercept update; a slope < 1, for logistic recalibration; the recalibrated version must in turn be evaluated.","The Hosmer-Lemeshow test compares observed and expected events by deciles of predicted risk: underpowered in small samples, it flags negligible departures in large ones. Judge instead from the smoothed calibration curve, the slope and the intercept."],t.s)
B.aZK=s(["Use the model as is.","Reduce overfitting (fewer parameters, shrinkage) and then validate externally."],t.s)
B.agK=new A.b0("Corrected AUC 0.84, corrected slope 0.78. What should be done before use?",B.aZK,1,null,0,"Slope 0.78: predictions too extreme; external validation is still needed.")
B.b6s=s([B.agK],t.Fe)
B.bke=s(["Reporting only the AUC.",'Randomly splitting the sample and calling it "external validation".',"Choosing the threshold on the same data without saying so."],t.s)
B.amg=new A.d0("calibration","Discrimination and calibration",B.cd,B.blX,"Cardiometabolic model (demonstration 3)","See the demonstration: apparent AUC, optimism-corrected AUC, corrected slope and decision curve.",B.b6s,B.bke,"prediction_logistic_internal")
B.blM=s(["Pearson's r measures the strength of a LINEAR relationship; Spearman's \u03c1 that of a monotonic relationship.","A strong correlation proves neither causality nor agreement between two methods (two methods can be perfectly correlated and differ by 20%).","Always look at the scatter plot: an extreme value can create or hide a correlation."],t.s)
B.b7j=s(["Pearson's r","Bland-Altman","Welch's t"],t.s)
B.ago=new A.b0("To judge whether two devices are interchangeable, one uses\u2026",B.b7j,1,null,0,"Agreement is studied on the differences (Bland-Altman).")
B.bo2=s([B.ago],t.Fe)
B.boF=s(["Concluding agreement from r.","Interpreting r = 0 as the absence of any relationship (a non-linear relationship is possible)."],t.s)
B.amh=new A.d0("correlation","Correlation: what it says and does not say",B.ee,B.blM,"Creatinine on two analysers","If analyser B always gives 1.2 \xd7 the value of A, r = 1 although the results are not interchangeable.",B.bo2,B.boF,"pearson")
B.bko=s(["For each subject, the difference between methods is computed. The bias is the mean of the differences; the limits of agreement (bias \xb1 1.96 SD) contain about 95% of individual differences.","The limits of agreement are NOT a confidence interval: they describe the spread of individuals. The CI of the bias describes the precision of the mean; each limit also has its own CI.","Acceptability is judged against a clinically tolerable difference set BEFORE the analysis.","If the difference increases with the measured level (proportional bias), analyse the ratio or the logarithm.","To compare a new assay with the reference method, Passing-Bablok (nonparametric) or Deming (error on both methods) regression separates constant bias (intercept \u2260 0) from proportional bias (slope \u2260 1); the conclusion rests on the bias at clinical decision levels, compared with an acceptable bias set a priori."],t.s)
B.b2r=s(["that the true bias lies between \u22120.9 and 1.3 with 95% confidence.","that about 95% of individual differences are expected between \u22120.9 and 1.3."],t.s)
B.ahs=new A.b0('"95% limits of agreement: \u22120.9 to 1.3" means\u2026',B.b2r,1,null,0,"The limits of agreement describe individuals; the CI of the bias describes the mean.")
B.amt=s([B.ahs],t.Fe)
B.az2=s(['Calling the limits of agreement a "confidence interval".',"Judging agreement without an a priori clinical criterion."],t.s)
B.ami=new A.d0("agreement","Agreement between methods: Bland-Altman",B.ef,B.bko,"Capillary vs venous blood glucose","Bias = +0.2 mmol/L (CI 0.05 to 0.35); limits of agreement \u22120.9 to +1.3 mmol/L: a patient may show a difference of 1.3 mmol/L, which a narrow CI of the bias does not show.",B.amt,B.az2,"bland_altman")
B.bo3=s(["Two measurements on the same subjects (before/after, two eyes, two tests on the same sample) are not independent: the analysis is about the within-subject differences.","Quantitative: paired t (differences ~ normal) or Wilcoxon signed-rank. Binary: McNemar, which uses only the discordant pairs.","Without a control group, a before/after change is not a treatment effect (regression to the mean, natural course)."],t.s)
B.ahq=new A.b0("How many pairs are used by McNemar's test in the example?",B.Q_,1,null,0,"14 + 5 = 19 discordant pairs.")
B.bjs=s([B.ahq],t.Fe)
B.bdp=s(["Analysing two measurement times as two independent groups.","Silently excluding incomplete pairs."],t.s)
B.amj=new A.d0("paired","Paired data",B.ef,B.bo3,"Two tests on 100 samples","40 positive with both, 41 negative with both, 14 positive only with test A, 5 only with test B: only the 19 discordant pairs inform the comparison.",B.bjs,B.bdp,"mcnemar")
B.bj3=s(["MCAR: missingness unrelated to the data; MAR: related to observed variables; MNAR: related to the missing value itself.","Complete-case analysis is unbiased under MCAR (and in some MAR cases) but loses power; otherwise it may be biased.","Always report the number of missing values per variable and compare included and excluded subjects.","Multiple imputation is the reference under MAR: m completed datasets, one analysis per dataset, pooling by Rubin's rules (the between-imputation variance reflects the uncertainty due to missing values). Include the outcome in the imputation model. Single mean imputation underestimates the variance.","For a prediction model, imputation must be redone in each bootstrap replicate; otherwise internal validation reuses information from the whole dataset."],t.s)
B.bgQ=s(["has no consequence.","artificially reduces the variance and can bias associations."],t.s)
B.ah6=new A.b0("Replacing missing values with the mean\u2026",B.bgQ,1,null,0,"Single imputation: illusory precision.")
B.amw=s([B.ah6],t.Fe)
B.aFA=s(["Not mentioning exclusions.","Imputing the outcome in prediction without declaring it."],t.s)
B.amk=new A.d0("missing","Missing data",B.ef,B.bj3,"Missing blood glucose","If blood glucose is missing mostly in patients seen in the emergency department, the MCAR assumption is implausible.",B.amw,B.aFA,"describe_numeric")
